  <iati-identifier>GB-EDU-133903-NIHR200125-SHARP</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="GB-EDU-133903" type="80">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving experiences of severe stigmatising skin diseases in Ghana and Ethiopia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="4">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SHARP aims to achieve the following impact and deliverables:
&#x2022; Transformative programme of work
&#x2022; Equitable partnerships and collaboration leading to capacity building and health system strengthening
&#x2022; Adaptable, transferable package of training, community engagement materials and implementation guidelines for cost effective SSSD case finding and treatment adherence support
&#x2022; Adaptable and transferable package of stigma-reduction materials.
&#x2022; Improved wound care package for BU
&#x2022; Evidence based outcome measures and treatment monitoring guidelines for CL treatment monitoring guidelines for CL</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SHARP objectives are to: 
1.  Explore the current capacity and organisation of the public health and local care systems to provide care for severe &amp; stigmatising skin conditions

2.  Understand both the lived experience of these skin conditions amongst communities and the local care system from the perspective of individuals
- To understand the economic burden of skin NTDs on patients and their households in Ghana and Ethiopia, in order to inform development of intervention strategies, evaluation methods and tools
- To explore how for each SSSD, stigma may be produced, experienced and mitigated in Ghana and Ethiopia

3. Co-develop an effective, acceptable and sustainable package of interventions</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Skin Health Africa Research Programme (SHARP)  aims to improve outcomes for people with severe stigmatising skin diseases (SSSDs) in Ghana and Ethiopia by strengthening the care cascade through scalable strategies for earlier detection, improving management to reduce disability and counteracting stigma and discrimination.&#xa0;

A Burulii Ulcer (BU) trial in Ghana will evaluate the performance of strategies to improve healing.
A Cutaneous Leishmaniasis (CL) cohort in Ethiopia will validate person-centred outcome measures to optimise care and inform evidence for treatment

SHARP is a collaboration between the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine (LSHTM) in the UK, the Noguchi Memorial Institute for Medical Research, University of Ghana (NMIMR UG) and the Kumasi Centre for Collaborative Research in Tropical Medicine of Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology (KCCR, KNUST) in Ghana, and the Armauer Hansen Research Institute (AHRI) and Addis Ababa University (AAU) in Ethiopia</narrative>
  </description>
